## Why This Is Asked

Tests distributed-systems thinking: exactly-once, dedup, backpressure, observability.

## Key Concepts

- Exactly-once processing
- Deduplication with dedup_id
- Backpressure strategies (batching, pause vs drop)
- Observability (tracing, metrics, logs)
- Kafka vs Kinesis trade-offs
- Idempotent sinks and compensating actions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode for idempotent sink write
function writeEvent(evt, sink, store) {
  const id = evt.dedup_id;
  if (store.seen(id)) return;
  sink.write(evt);
  store.markSeen(id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test guarantees for partial failures?
- How would you ensure exactly-once with at-least-once queues?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design scalable feature flags with per-user rollout, regional controls, and safe hot flips.

## Key Concepts
- Global distribution and eventual consistency
- Data modeling for flags and segments
- Cache invalidation and TTL
- Rollout semantics and hot-flip safety

## Code Example
```javascript
function evaluateFeature(user, flag, context){
  const bucket = hash(user.id + flag.name) % 100;
  const inSegment = (flag.segments && flag.segments.length) ? flag.segments.includes(user.segment) : true;
  const regionOK = flag.regionOverride ? user.region === flag.regionOverride : true;
  return (bucket < flag.rolloutPct) && inSegment && regionOK;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate rollout accuracy under traffic spikes?
- How would you rollback a faulty flag without service interruption?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of simple rate limiting, memory bounds, and concurrency in a real service.

## Key Concepts

- In-memory data structures for per-key state
- Time-window calculations and pruning
- Concurrency and process-safety; scalability trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
const buckets = new Map();

function limit(req, res, next){
  const key = req.headers['x-api-key'];
  if(!key) return res.status(400).send('API key required');
  const now = Date.now();
  const arr = buckets.get(key) || [];
  // prune
  while (arr.length && now - arr[0] > 10 * 60 * 1000) arr.shift();
  if (arr.length >= 100) return res.status(429).send('Too many requests');
  arr.push(now);
  buckets.set(key, arr);
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a multi-instance deployment?
- What are the limitations of in-memory rate limiting?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to diagnose and execute a safe, low-downtime rollout when a new image introduces instability. Focuses on rollout metadata, readiness, and quick rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes Deployments and ReplicaSets
- Rollout history and remediation
- Readiness/Liveness probes and startup checks
- Canary/blue-green deployment patterns for safety
- Rollback with kubectl rollout undo
- Latency validation under load

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js pseudo-code using Kubernetes client to check rollout and query metrics
const k8s = require('@kubernetes/client-node');
const kc = new k8s.KubeConfig();
kc.loadFromDefault();
const apps = kc.makeApiClient(k8s.AppsV1Api);
async function checkRollout(name, ns='default') {
  const r = await apps.readNamespacedDeployment(name, ns);
  console.log(r.body.status);
}
checkRollout('orders-api', 'kcsa-prod');
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you explicitly structure a canary deployment to guarantee no more than 10% traffic to the new version while monitoring SLI/SLOs?
- What metrics and alerts would you rely on to detect regressions during rollout and trigger rollback automatically?
